cleaning_operations:
The following coding systems were developed and introduced for Belarus household sample survey: 
1) coding of households covered by the survey;
2) coding of household expenditures;
3) coding of additional incomes and employment of household members by sectors and types of activity. 

Detailed information about coding procedures is available in ""Belarus Household Budget Survey Methodology"" (p.22-25) in Technical Documents.

sampling_procedure:
The Household Sample Survey is administered to 6000 households on a yearly basis in all regions of the Republic of Belarus and Minsk City covering 49 cities, towns and urban-type settlements as well as 53 rural Councils. Sampled households are surveyed for a year and then are subject to replacement (rotation).

For the purpose of household survey in the Republic of Belarus, the subject of survey is understood as the total households of the country excluding: 
1) persons fully supported by the state, for example persons staying in homes for the elderly and the disabled, children in public care institutions, prisoners and etc; 
2) foreigners living and working in Belarus on contract basis;
3) families of military men living in military residential settlements or other restricted areas. 

A household is understood as the unit of the survey. This is a person or a group of persons (mainly relatives) living together and having a common budget. Close relationship is not an obligatory condition. 
For survey purposes the category ""households"" includes: 
a) families consisting of a husband and wife with or without children or single parent families; 
b) relatives living together and having a common budget (brother and sister, grandmother and grandson and etc.); 
c) persons living together and having a common budget but who are not relatives, for example two friends; 
d) persons living alone; 
e) families consisting of two and more married couples with or without children. 

The size of sample was defined depending on the survey tasks and degree of diversity of the general total in terms of the surveyed characteristics and available financial resources. Given all these factors, the size of sample was determined as 6000 households or 0.2% of the total number of households in the country. 

Sampling was based on the territorial principle of forming a selection. In accordance with this principle, sampling involved three stages; different territorial units of selection were used at every stage: the larger units were used at the initial stage while at the third stage the surveyed unit was equal to the selection unit. To ensure equal distribution of sample over the territory of the country, the selection was made separately for urban and rural population.

Detailed information about sampling procedures could be found in ""Belarus Household Sample Survey Methodology"" (p. 9-17) in external resources.

The Household Sample Survey is administered to 6000 households on a yearly basis in all regions of the Republic of Belarus and Minsk City covering 49 cities, towns and urban-type settlements as well as 53 rural Councils. Sampled households are surveyed for a year and then are subject to replacement (rotation).

For the purpose of household survey in the Republic of Belarus, the subject of survey is understood as the total households of the country excluding: 
1) persons fully supported by the state, for example persons staying in homes for the elderly and the disabled, children in public care institutions, prisoners and etc; 
2) foreigners living and working in Belarus on contract basis;
3) families of military men living in military residential settlements or other restricted areas. 

A household is understood as the unit of the survey. This is a person or a group of persons (mainly relatives) living together and having a common budget. Close relationship is not an obligatory condition. 
For survey purposes the category ""households"" includes: 
a) families consisting of a husband and wife with or without children or single parent families; 
b) relatives living together and having a common budget (brother and sister, grandmother and grandson and etc.); 
c) persons living together and having a common budget but who are not relatives, for example two friends; 
d) persons living alone; 
e) families consisting of two and more married couples with or without children. 

The size of sample was defined depending on the survey tasks and degree of diversity of the general total in terms of the surveyed characteristics and available financial resources. Given all these factors, the size of sample was determined as 6000 households or 0.2% of the total number of households in the country. 

Sampling was based on the territorial principle of forming a selection. In accordance with this principle, sampling involved three stages; different territorial units of selection were used at every stage: the larger units were used at the initial stage while at the third stage the surveyed unit was equal to the selection unit. To ensure equal distribution of sample over the territory of the country, the selection was made separately for urban and rural population.

Detailed information about sampling procedures could be found in ""Belarus Household Sample Survey Methodology"" (p. 9-17) in external resources.

